! An oxygen molecule with its bond stretched: a force in a magnetic system.
!
! O2 is a triplet -- two unpaired electrons in the pi* orbitals -- so this is a
! genuinely spin-polarized force rather than an nspin = 2 run that converges to
! the unpolarized answer. The bond is set to 2.5 bohr, past the equilibrium 2.28,
! so the force pulls the atoms together and is large enough to compare.
!
! The trap it exists for: QE's `force_lc` is handed `rho%of_r(:,1)`, and in the
! (up, down) storage this code uses that is *one channel*, not the total charge.
! A local force built from half the density is halved, and nothing in an
! unpolarized test can see it. The PAW dataset adds the one-centre terms to the
! same check.
!
! The molecule is in a 12 bohr box at Gamma, which is a molecule-in-a-periodic-
! cell rather than an isolated molecule -- what matters here is that both codes
! solve the same problem, not that the box is converged.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tprnfor = .true.
    verbosity = 'high'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=1, celldm(1)=12.0, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    nspin=2, starting_magnetization(1)=0.5,
    ecutwfc=30.0, ecutrho=180.0,
    occupations='smearing', smearing='gaussian', degauss=0.02
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 O  15.999  O.pz-kjpaw.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (bohr)
 O 0.00 0.00 0.00
 O 2.50 0.00 0.00
K_POINTS (tpiba)
 1
 0.0 0.0 0.0 1.0
